; PT10, point 2, and PT4, point P11: grips on the vertices. Equal vertices
; (exact equality of the data) have one grip, and moving it moves them all;
; a grip that would bring all four vertices to one point is refused, as the
; reader leaves such a SOLID unhandled. Values by hand.
Q = SOLID vertex=0,0 vertex=4,0 vertex=0,2 vertex=4,2
T = SOLID vertex=0,0 vertex=4,0 vertex=0,3 vertex=0,3
S = SOLID vertex=0,0 vertex=0,0 vertex=0,2 vertex=4,2
L = SOLID vertex=0,0 vertex=0,0 vertex=5,5 vertex=5,5
P = SOLID vertex=0,0 vertex=0,0 vertex=0,0 vertex=3,0
? grips Q -> 0,0; 4,0; 0,2; 4,2
? grip Q 4,2 5,3 -> SOLID vertex=0,0 vertex=4,0 vertex=0,2 vertex=5,3
? grip Q 0,0 -1,-1 -> SOLID vertex=-1,-1 vertex=4,0 vertex=0,2 vertex=4,2
? grip Q 2,1 3,3 -> none
; Three vertices: v2 and v3 share one grip.
? grips T -> 0,0; 4,0; 0,3
? grip T 0,3 1,4 -> SOLID vertex=0,0 vertex=4,0 vertex=1,4 vertex=1,4
; v0 = v1: one grip, which moves both.
? grips S -> 0,0; 0,2; 4,2
? grip S 0,0 1,-1 -> SOLID vertex=1,-1 vertex=1,-1 vertex=0,2 vertex=4,2
; Moving a single vertex onto another is allowed while the four are not all equal.
? grip S 4,2 0,2 -> SOLID vertex=0,0 vertex=0,0 vertex=0,2 vertex=0,2
; Two pairs: moving one pair onto the other would make all four equal.
? grips L -> 0,0; 5,5
? grip L 5,5 0,0 -> invalid
? grip L 0,0 5,5 -> invalid
? grip L 5,5 1,0 -> SOLID vertex=0,0 vertex=0,0 vertex=1,0 vertex=1,0
; Three equal vertices and one apart.
? grips P -> 0,0; 3,0
? grip P 3,0 0,0 -> invalid
? grip P 0,0 3,0 -> invalid
? grip P 3,0 6,0 -> SOLID vertex=0,0 vertex=0,0 vertex=0,0 vertex=6,0
